{{Short description|Extinct group of marine invertebrates}}
'''Pelmatozoa''' is a possible [[clade]] or [[evolutionary grade|grade]] within the phylum [[Echinodermata]].  As originally defined by [[Rudolph Leuckart]]  its earliest form, it was a subphylum including all sedentary echinoderms including non-stalked groups such as [[Edrioasteroidea]].<ref name=bather>{{harvnb|Bather|1900}}</ref>  The definition of the group has changed over time, and recent proponents of Pelmatozoa as a monophyletic clade ***TODO: FINISH***

In [[Francis Arthur Bather|F. A. Bather's]] influential two-subphylum taxonomy published in 1900,<ref>{{harvnb|Smith|1984|pp=433–434}}</ref> Pelmatozoa encompassed all ancestrally sedentary echinoderms including the non-stalked [[Edrioasteroidea]] and [[Homalozoa]].<ref name=bather />  Later taxonomies broke up this grouping in various ways.<ref>{{harvnb|Moore|1966}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Ubaghs|1967}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Sprinkle|1980a}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Sprinkle|1980b}}</ref> As of 2024, both of the major theories of echinoderm phylogenetics could allow for a [[paraphyletic]] Pelmatozoa, depending on which recently-discovered groups are included, and on how certain [[polytomies]] are resolved.<ref>{{harvnb|David|Mooi|1999|p=100}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|David|Lefebvre|Mooi|Parsley|2000|p=549}}</ref><ref name=overview>{{harvnb|Rahman|Zamora|2024|p=310}}</ref>  The [[Crinoidea]] are the only extant pelmatozoan group.<ref name=bather />

